WEBVTT

0
00:00:00.509 --> 00:00:03.029
To effectively configure your Workato automation,

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:05.329
begin by defining the structure of your data.

2
00:00:05.590 --> 00:00:08.159
Creating a clear sample JSON payload is

3
00:00:08.159 --> 00:00:10.479
the first essential step in building dynamic,

4
00:00:10.640 --> 00:00:12.619
scalable API integrations.

5
00:00:13.000 --> 00:00:18.059
Here we see a dataable named computer_request_approval.

6
00:00:18.159 --> 00:00:21.319
It captures critical information like requester emails,

7
00:00:21.525 --> 00:00:22.475
Device details,

8
00:00:22.725 --> 00:00:23.236
costs,

9
00:00:23.406 --> 00:00:24.676
and approval statuses,

10
00:00:24.926 --> 00:00:27.805
all of which serve as the foundation for our JSON payload.

11
00:00:28.246 --> 00:00:30.565
When mapping a single dataable road to JSON,

12
00:00:30.805 --> 00:00:33.625
we create a flat structure that mirrors our column headers.

13
00:00:33.816 --> 00:00:36.686
This ensures that every individual data point is

14
00:00:36.686 --> 00:00:39.675
correctly represented and ready for API processing.

15
00:00:40.015 --> 00:00:42.526
Since managers often need to review multiple requests.

16
00:00:42.731 --> 00:00:43.242
At once,

17
00:00:43.411 --> 00:00:44.841
we must upgrade our schema.

18
00:00:45.012 --> 00:00:49.591
By wrapping our data in a requests array and adding a record underscore ID,

19
00:00:49.692 --> 00:00:52.602
we support batch operations and unique item tracking.

20
00:00:52.891 --> 00:00:53.391
Finally,

21
00:00:53.611 --> 00:00:55.192
bring your payload into work auto.

22
00:00:55.451 --> 00:00:58.332
Use the paste JSON sample feature to automatically

23
00:00:58.332 --> 00:01:00.242
generate your trigger schema and data pills,

24
00:01:00.451 --> 00:01:03.652
instantly preparing your workflow to handle the dynamic data you've just defined.